\begin{proof}
Let $2^s$ be the largest power of $2$ not exceeding $n$, so $2^s\le n<2^{s+1}$. Put the sum over the common denominator $2^s\,\mathrm{lcm}(1,2,\ldots,n)$, or equivalently use the $2$-adic valuation of its reduced numerator. Among the terms $1/2,1/3,\ldots,1/n$, the unique term whose denominator has the largest power of $2$ is $1/2^s$ (every other denominator has $2$-adic valuation at most $s-1$). After multiplying by $2^s$, the contribution of $1/2^s$ is odd, while every other contribution is even. Hence the numerator of the whole sum, when expressed with a denominator whose $2$-part is $2^s$, is odd, so the reduced denominator is divisible by $2$. Therefore the sum cannot be an integer.
\end{proof}
